│       commercial-yeast-vs-sou… ││   Great bread is a small set of processes happening in the same bowl: starches     │
│       kneading-vs-folding      ││   hydrating, proteins linking into gluten, yeasts producing gas, and heat finally  │
│   ▾ concepts                   ││   setting the crumb. Understanding each process in isolation makes most recipes    │
│       autolyse                 ││   legible, because nearly every instruction in a bread recipe exists to favour one │
│       windowpane-test          ││   of these processes over another.                                                 │
│   ▾ entities                   ││                                                                                    │
│       banneton                 ││   Flour, water and time                                                            │
│       dutch-oven               ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Wheat flour carries two storage proteins, glutenin and gliadin. When water is    │
│                                ││   added and the flour rests, they unwind and begin to bond into an elastic         │
│                                ││   network. Given time and gentle handling that network develops on its own, which  │
│                                ││   is why a long rest can replace much of the hard kneading a recipe asks for.      │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Enzymes in the flour, mainly amylase, slowly cut starches into sugars. Those     │
│                                ││   sugars feed the yeast and, later, sweeten the crust through browning. A dough    │
│                                ││   that rests longer tastes faintly sweeter and browns deeper for exactly this      │
│                                ││   reason.                                                                          │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Gas, structure and heat                                                          │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Fermentation produces carbon dioxide that collects inside the gluten network. In │
│                                ││   the oven the gas expands, the loaf springs upward, and heat sets the crumb,      │
│                                ││   turning a soft paste into a sliceable structure. The baker's job is less about   │
│                                ││   forcing any of this and more about timing it.                                    │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Ratios                                                                           │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
│                                ││   Professional bakers describe every formula as a set of percentages of the flour  │
│                                ││   weight. Writing a recipe that way makes hydration explicit and lets a baker      │
│                                ││   scale the formula to any amount of flour without arithmetic surprises.           │
│                                ││                                                                                    │
